"CustomerSummary": "Two senior customers (over 65, retired, with moderate risk tolerance) were affected by the broker's recommendations of 25 short-term switches of Class A mutual funds between July 2018 and September 2021. These Class A funds typically carry substantial front-end loads and are intended for long-term holding. The broker recommended these switches without a reasonable basis to believe they were in the customers' best interest, with average holding periods of only 229-273 days. The Care Obligation violation occurred because the broker: (1) failed to consider the customers' investment objectives and time horizons; (2) failed to consider the costs associated with the recommendations; (3) failed to consider reasonably available alternatives that would have achieved the customers' objectives at lower cost; and (4) placed his own interests ahead of the customers by generating commission revenue through the excessive switching. Customer A suffered $16,817.64 in excessive fees and Customer B suffered $14,857.50 in excessive fees, for which restitution was ordered.",

"OverallSummary": "This case involves a broker who violated the Care Obligation of Regulation Best Interest by recommending and executing 25 short-term switches of Class A mutual funds to two senior customers between July 2018 and September 2021. The broker's recommendations were unsuitable and not in the customers' best interest because: (1) Class A mutual funds are designed as long-term investments due to their substantial front-end loads; (2) the average holding period was only 229-273 days, far too short to justify the sales charges; (3) the broker created a pattern of switching between different mutual fund families rather than within the same fund family, which would have reduced costs; (4) the broker failed to consider the customers' investment objectives, time horizons, and moderate risk tolerance; (5) the broker failed to consider reasonably available alternatives at lower cost; and (6) the broker generated commissions at the expense of his customers. The two affected customers paid a total of $40,972.50 in excessive sales fees, with restitution of $31,675.14 ordered (the remainder having been paid by the broker's firm). This case highlights how brokers must consider costs, holding periods, and reasonably available alternatives when recommending products with significant front-end loads to ensure recommendations are truly in customers' best interests."
